[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Comparison-Based Sorting: Bubble Sort, Insertion Sort, Selection Sort"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Comparison-based sorting algorithms rely on comparing elements to determine their relative order. This tutorial explores three fundamental comparison-based sorting algorithms: Bubble Sort, Insertion Sort, and Selection Sort. We will analyze their principles, implementations, and performance characteristics."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Bubble Sort"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"Bubble Sort repeatedly steps through the list, compares adjacent elements, and swaps them if they are in the wrong order. The pass through the list is repeated until no swaps are needed, which indicates that the list is sorted."},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"function bubbleSort(arr) {
  let n = arr.length;
  let swapped;
  do {
    swapped = false;
    for (let i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
      if (arr[i] > arr[i + 1]) {
        // Swap arr[i] and arr[i+1]
        let temp = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[i + 1];
        arr[i + 1] = temp;
        swapped = true;
      }
    }
    n--;
  } while (swapped);
  return arr;
}"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Bubble Sort's time complexity is O(n^2) in the average and worst cases, making it inefficient for large datasets. However, it is simple to implement and performs well on nearly sorted data (O(n) in the best case)."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Insertion Sort"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Insertion Sort builds the final sorted array one item at a time. It iterates through the input array, and for each element, it inserts it into its correct position in the already sorted portion of the array."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"function insertionSort(arr) {
  let n = arr.length;
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    let key = arr[i];
    let j = i - 1;
    while (j >= 0 && arr[j] > key) {
      arr[j + 1] = arr[j];
      j = j - 1;
    }
    arr[j + 1] = key;
  }
  return arr;
}"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Insertion Sort has a time complexity of O(n^2) in the average and worst cases. It performs well on small datasets or nearly sorted data. It is also an in-place sorting algorithm, meaning it requires minimal additional memory."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Selection Sort"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Selection Sort divides the input array into two parts: the sorted subarray and the unsorted subarray. It repeatedly finds the minimum element from the unsorted subarray and swaps it with the first element of the unsorted subarray, effectively expanding the sorted subarray."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"function selectionSort(arr) {
  let n = arr.length;
  for (let i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
    let minIndex = i;
    for (let j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
      if (arr[j] < arr[minIndex]) {
        minIndex = j;
      }
    }
    if (minIndex !== i) {
      // Swap arr[i] and arr[minIndex]
      let temp = arr[i];
      arr[i] = arr[minIndex];
      arr[minIndex] = temp;
    }
  }
  return arr;
}"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Selection Sort has a time complexity of O(n^2) in all cases (best, average, and worst). It performs poorly on large datasets. However, it has the advantage of minimizing the number of swaps, making it useful when memory write operations are costly."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Comparison Summary"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Bubble Sort: Simple to implement, but inefficient for large datasets (O(n^2)). Best case O(n) when nearly sorted."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Insertion Sort: Efficient for small datasets or nearly sorted data (O(n^2)). In-place sorting."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Selection Sort: Consistent performance (O(n^2)), minimizes swaps. Not efficient for large datasets."},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Bubble Sort, Insertion Sort, and Selection Sort are fundamental comparison-based sorting algorithms. While they are easy to understand and implement, their quadratic time complexity makes them unsuitable for large datasets. Understanding these algorithms provides a foundation for learning more advanced and efficient sorting techniques."}]
